<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>You don't need to memorize every code — read the first digit, or count the blinks.</h2>

    <p><strong>Whirlpool dishwasher codes use an <span class="code">F#E#</span> pattern, and the <span class="hl">F-digit tells you the system</span>, stable across models: F6 = fill/water in, F7 = heat, F8 = drain, F9 = diverter, FA (F10) = dispenser/vent/dry, F1 = control, F2 = user interface, F3 = water sensor, F5 = door. The E-digit narrows it and can vary by model year. And if your dishwasher has no number display, <span class="hl">the code blinks out on the Clean light</span> — count the flashes. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>That family logic is the whole trick. A code you've never seen — say F6E7 — you can still place: F6 is the fill side, so it's a water-in fault (it's the flow meter, as it happens). Below we give the family key, how to read blink sequences, and the full confirmed table. Two codes are worth flagging up front: F2E2 is a communication error, not a leak; and F0E0 is not a real Whirlpool dishwasher code, whatever some charts claim.</p>

    <p>Everything here applies to <strong>KitchenAid, Maytag, and JennAir</strong> too — same Whirlpool platform. Parent reference: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">all dishwasher error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/whirlpool-dishwasher-repair/">Whirlpool dishwasher repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The family key</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Read the F-digit first — it's the reliable part.</h2>
    <p class="lead">The F-family meaning holds across model years. The specific E-suffix varies by control-board generation, so treat the family as certain and the sub-code as "which part in that system."</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>System</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>F1</td><td>Control board</td><td>Stuck relay (F1E1), failed board (F1E2)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F2</td><td>User interface</td><td>Stuck button (F2E1), UI-to-control comm (F2E2)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F3</td><td>Thermistor / OWI sensor</td><td>Sensor failure/short, calibration (F3E1/E2/E3)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F4</td><td>Pump communication</td><td>Control-to-circulation-pump comm (F4E3)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F5</td><td>Door</td><td>Door switch / won't open (F5E1/E2)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F6</td><td>Fill / water in</td><td>Won't fill, inlet valve, float, cold water, flow meter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F7</td><td>Heat</td><td>Element doesn't work (F7E1) / won't shut off (F7E2)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F8</td><td>Drain</td><td>Slow drain (F8E1), drain pump (F8E2/E3)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F9</td><td>Diverter</td><td>Disc position/motor (F9E1/E2/E3)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FA / F10</td><td>Dispenser / vent / dry</td><td>Detergent dispenser, vent wax-motor, drying fan</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Not a Whirlpool dishwasher code:</strong> F0E0 (not in the published set). Also there's a <span class="code">H2O</span> notice = low/no water supply.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">No display?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Read the blink sequence on the Clean light.</h2>
    <p>Many Whirlpool-family dishwashers don't have a number readout — the fault blinks out on the Clean (or Heavy) light instead. The flash pattern encodes the F#E# number: a group of flashes, a pause, then another group, corresponding to the two digits. Watch a couple of full cycles to nail the count, then match it to the family key above. It's documented Whirlpool behavior, not guesswork — the light IS the code. If the pattern is hard to read, describe what the light is doing when you call and we'll translate it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Full code table</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Confirmed Whirlpool / KitchenAid / Maytag F#E# codes.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>F1E1 / F1E2</td><td>Stuck relay / failed control board</td><td>Main control board</td></tr>
