</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Drain &amp; heat</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">F8 goes to the drain guide; F7 to the drying guide.</h2>
    <p><strong>F8 (drain):</strong> F8E1 is a slow drain, F8E2/F8E3 are drain-pump electrical faults. Start at the filter, drain hose, air gap, and the disposal knockout plug on new installs. The full step-by-step drain walkthrough is on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-not-draining/">Whirlpool dishwasher not draining</a> page — F8 codes point there for the fix.</p>
    <p><strong>F7 (heat):</strong> F7E1 is a dead heating element, F7E2 is an element that won't shut off. On heated-dry models a dead element also leaves dishes wet — if that's your symptom, our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-drying/">dishwasher not drying</a> page covers the heated-dry vs condensation picture. Element/thermostat work runs $180 to $460.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Shared platform</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">KitchenAid, Maytag, and JennAir use these same codes.</h2>
    <p>All four brands run the Whirlpool Corporation F#E# platform, so the family key and the table above apply across them, with parts from shared catalog families. A specific KitchenAid or Maytag model's booklet may not reprint every code — verify a code against that model if it's not listed — but the family meanings carry straight over. Our techs treat them as one platform for diagnosis and parts.</p>
    <p>Related: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-not-draining/">Whirlpool not draining</a> · <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/kitchenaid-not-draining/">KitchenAid not draining</a> · <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/maytag-not-draining/">Maytag not draining</a> · brand pillars <a href="/brands/kitchenaid-dishwasher-repair/">KitchenAid</a> · <a href="/brands/maytag-dishwasher-repair/">Maytag</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Cut power at the breaker (or unplug) for a minute, restore, run a short cycle. For F2E2, Whirlpool's step is a 30-second power-off.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the blink sequence</strong> if there's no display — count the Clean-light flashes to get the F#E# number.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the filter (F8E1).</strong> Twist out the filter cylinder at the tub bottom, rinse, reinstall.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the drain path (F8).</strong> Hose kinks, air gap, disposal knockout plug on new installs.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the supply (F6E1).</strong> Valve open, fill hose not kinked, inlet screen clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the hot tap (F6E6).</strong> Cold fill water can trip the code.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Control (F1), thermistor (F3), pump comm (F4), heater (F7), diverter (F9), and flow meter (F6E7) codes that return every cycle are real hardware faults — book a visit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A young machine with one code is worth fixing; a diverter or board fault on an old one is a conversation.</h2>
    <p>A single fill, drain, or door code on a Whirlpool-family dishwasher in its first several years is a straightforward, bounded repair. The place we'll tell you to weigh replacement is a control-board (F1) or diverter (F9) failure on an older unit that's also throwing other codes — those parts plus labor can approach a new mid-range machine. We give you that math at the door instead of just installing the part. $89 diagnostic, waived if you go ahead with the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

